UnScribble — Because "I Think That Says Amoxicillin" Is Not a Medical Standard

---

THE PROBLEM

Every year, roughly 7,000 Americans die from medication errors caused by illegible handwriting.

Not a rare disease. Not a complex drug interaction. A bad loop on the letter "a."

The IOM flagged this in 1999. The fax machine was already 15 years old. The internet existed. And people were still dying because a pharmacist had to guess.

Electronic prescriptions exist now — but 30% of prescriptions in the US are still handwritten. Rural clinics. Urgent care. Developing countries. The problem didn't go away. We just stopped talking about it.

---

THE SOLUTION

UnScribble takes a photo of a handwritten prescription and returns structured, verified, human-readable data — streamed live while you watch seven AI agents work through the chaos.

Not raw OCR. Not "here's some text, good luck." A full extraction pipeline with patient info, medications, dosages, schedules, safety flags, and a plain-English summary — all in strict JSON with a schema that doesn't let the model freestyle.


HOW IT WORKS

Take a photo of a handwritten prescription. UnScribble runs it through a 7-agent AI pipeline and returns structured, validated medication data — patient info, drug names, dosages, frequencies, safety flags — in seconds.

Not a text dump. Not raw OCR. A clean JSON contract with confidence scores, formulary-validated drug names, and explicit flags for anything a human should double-check before dispensing.


THE PIPELINE

Seven agents. Each one does one job.

The first agent looks at the image and decides if it's even worth processing. Bad lighting, motion blur, too much glare — it exits early rather than hallucinate through unusable input.

The second agent reads the raw text. It also infers regional handwriting style, because "QD" means something different depending on where the prescription was written.

Three agents run in parallel: one extracts patient demographics, one parses medication lines, one pulls allergies and clinical notes. Parallel because waiting is waste.

If the medication agent isn't confident enough in its own output — below 0.7 — it runs again. The two passes get merged using a self-consistency algorithm that picks the higher-confidence reading for each field.

Then a safety agent runs deterministic rules. Not vibes. Rules. Low-confidence flags, high-risk abbreviation checks, completeness checks, unresolved-token checks, structured human-review reasons.

Finally, a synthesis agent writes a plain-English summary a patient can actually read.

---

THE SAFETY LAYER

The hardest part of medical AI isn't extraction. It's knowing when not to trust the extraction.

UnScribble keeps the original OCR text alongside every corrected drug name. It fuzzy-matches against 864 formulary entries and auto-corrects only when the match is unambiguous. It also supplies 83 look-alike/sound-alike drug pairs to the medication extraction and second-pass review prompts, so confusing names like Metformin and Metronidazole get extra scrutiny instead of being treated like generic text.

When something is uncertain, it doesn't guess silently. It sets requires_human_review: true and tells you exactly why.

---

BEYOND THE DECODE

Once the prescription is decoded, the patient gets more than a list of drug names.

A visual medication schedule shows when to take what, with Google Calendar and ICS export. An interactive body map shows where each drug acts. A mechanism explorer explains what the drug actually does in plain language.

The goal isn't just to read the prescription. It's to make the prescription useful to the person it was written for.

---

THE BUILD

Vanilla JS. Node.js. One runtime dependency. No bundler, no framework, no build step.

46 unit tests. 7 end-to-end smoke tests. A mock mode that runs the full UI without an API key. A content-hash LRU cache that skips reprocessing identical images.

SSE streaming so every agent's progress is visible in real time — because in healthcare, transparency isn't optional.


Built solo. NVIDIA NIM. Vanilla JS. Seven agents. One dependency. One weekend.

And a 25-year-old statistic that really should have been fixed by now.
